<article class="env-proposition" id="7A85">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/7A85">Proposition <span data-tag="7A85">5.5.4.15</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be a presentable $\infty $-category, and let $S$ be a $($small$)$ collection of morphisms of $\calC $. Let $\overline{S}$ denote the strongly saturated class of morphisms generated by $S$. Let $\calC ' \subseteq \calC $ denote the full subcategory of $\calC $ consisting of $S$-local objects. Then: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
For each object $C \in \calC $, there exists a morphism $s: C \rightarrow C'$ such that $C'$ is $S$-local and $s$ belongs to $\overline{S}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calC '$ is presentable. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
The inclusion $\calC ' \subseteq \calC $ has a left adjoint $L$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(4)$"><p>
For every morphism $f$ of $\calC $, the following are equivalent: 
</p>
<ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
The morphism $f$ is an $S$-equivalence. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
The morphism $f$ belongs to $\overline{S}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iii)$"><p>
The induced morphism $Lf$ is an equivalence. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertion $(1)$ is a consequence of Lemma <a href="/tag/E7CE" data-tag="E7CE">5.5.5.14</a>, which we will prove in §<a href="/tag/78D7" data-tag="78D7">5.5.5</a>. The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ follows immediately from Proposition <a href="/tag/1349" data-tag="1349">5.2.7.8</a>. We now prove $(4)$. Lemma <a href="/tag/0621" data-tag="0621">5.5.4.11</a> implies that the collection of $S$-equivalences is a strongly saturated class of morphisms containing $S$; it therefore contains $\overline{S}$, so that $(ii) \Rightarrow (i)$. Now suppose that $f: X \rightarrow Y$ is such that $Lf$ is an equivalence, and consider the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X \ar [r] \ar [dr] \ar [d] &  Y \ar [d] \\ LX \ar [r] &  LY. } \]
</div>
    <p>
       Our proof of $(1)$ shows that the vertical morphisms belong to $\overline{S}$, and the lower horizontal arrow belongs to $\overline{S}$ by Remark <a href="/tag/5102" data-tag="5102">5.5.4.6</a>. Two applications of the two-out-of-three property now show that $f \in \overline{S}$, so that $(iii) \Rightarrow (ii)$. If $f$ is an $S$-equivalence, then we may again use the above diagram and the two-out-of-three property to conclude that $Lf$ is an equivalence. It follows that $LX$ and $LY$ co-represent the same functor on the homotopy category $h\calC '$, so that Yoneda's lemma implies that $Lf$ is an equivalence. Thus $(i) \Rightarrow (iii)$ and the proof of $(4)$ is complete. 
    <p>
      It remains to prove $(2)$. Remark <a href="/tag/3C20" data-tag="3C20">5.2.7.5</a> implies that $L \calC $ admits small colimits, so it will suffice to prove that $L \calC $ is accessible. According to Proposition <a href="/tag/379D" data-tag="379D">5.5.4.2</a>, this follows from the implication $(iii) \Rightarrow (i)$ of assertion $(4)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>